{
  "requirement": "In Markdown, escaped roles and role-looking text in inline code, fenced code, or raw HTML MUST remain literal and MUST NOT count as references. Raw HTML means an HTML block, an HTML comment, or an inline HTML tag including its attributes; text between inline tags is ordinary Markdown. A `{req}` directive inside a fenced code example and a legacy inline `{req}` role MUST NOT define a requirement.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "I judge the tests adequate for `requirements.references.markdown_literals`. Each obligation below has a test that exercises it and asserts the outcome.\n\n**Escaped roles stay literal**\n- `src/lib.rs#mod[tests]/fn[markdown_examples_and_escaped_references_stay_literal]` runs an escaped role through `preprocess_book`. It also checks that an escaped backslash does not escape the role: `\\\\{marker}` and `\\\\\\{marker}` give exactly one reference, at the expected offset.\n- `src/evidence.rs#mod[tests]/fn[every_markdown_extension_keeps_escaped_and_code_roles_literal]` covers `\\{cite}` in collection. `src/terms.rs#mod[tests]/fn[term_dependencies_of_every_markdown_extension_skip_literal_roles]` covers `\\{term}`.\n\n**Inline code and fenced code stay literal**\n- The same tests cover inline code and fenced code. For collection, `every_markdown_extension_keeps_escaped_and_code_roles_literal` also checks that a `:::{cite}` directive inside a code fence is not a reference.\n- `src/lib.rs#mod[tests]/fn[citation_metadata_preserves_prose_other_roles_and_literal_contexts]` covers backtick and tilde fences and inline code for fenced-citation handling. The parser-based `protected_ranges` handles fences uniformly, so I see no separate fence-style branch that needs its own test.\n\n**Raw HTML stays literal**\n- `roles_in_raw_html_stay_literal` covers an HTML block, an HTML comment, and an inline tag's attribute. It asserts exactly one reference survives (the one between `<em>` tags) and that the rendered book keeps the other four literal.\n- It also asserts that the same text in a non-Markdown context is not protected.\n- `src/fenced.rs#mod[tests]/fn[markdown_fences_in_raw_html_stay_literal]` covers colon-fence references inside a block, a comment, and a multi-line tag attribute. Only the fence outside HTML counts, and plain text counts all four.\n- `every_markdown_extension_keeps_escaped_and_code_roles_literal` also covers an attribute.\n\n**Every Markdown extension**\n- Collection and term dependencies are tested for `.md`, `.markdown`, `.MD`, and `.Markdown`, with `.txt` as a contrast showing that non-Markdown files are not protected.\n\n**`{req}` handling**\n- `code_examples_and_legacy_roles_remain_literal` and `colon_directives_render_markdown_bodies_and_legacy_roles_stay_literal` cover a `{req}` directive in a code fence and an inline `{req}` role. The first shows `:::{req} block` stays literal. Both show `{req}`legacy`` and `{req}`inline`` define nothing and render no badge, while a real `:::{req}` directive still defines a requirement.\n- `src/evidence.rs` tests that run through `collect_definitions` also include fenced `{req}` and `{example}` text inside definition bodies. Collection shares `definition_blocks` with the rendering path, so the fenced case is covered on both paths.\n\n**Scanning requirement bodies and term references**\n- `references_in_requirement_bodies_are_scanned_as_markdown` checks that a code-fenced role inside a `{req}` body is skipped while a prose role in that body counts.\n- `tests/definitions.rs#fn[literal_term_roles_in_subject_and_requirements_inputs_need_no_definition]` checks that literal `{term}` roles (inline code, escaped, HTML attribute, fenced) need no definition. It also shows that the same role written as a real reference does require one.\n\nI found no plausible requirement-violating behavior that these tests would accept, and no relevant `{fixme}` acknowledgments.",
